         __m128i tmp = _mm_slli_si128? Let's use the well-tested version:

       From https://www.intel.com/content/dam/www/public/us/en/documents/white-papers/carry-less-multiplication-instruction-paper.pdf
       and common implementations (cryptogams / openssl style):

         v = X0
         v = v ^ (v >> 1) ^ (v >> 2) ^ (v >> 7)   shifted within the low part into high... 
       I'll use an explicitly correct bit implementation for combination (few blocks)
       and a fast one for the bulk, verified by test.
    */
    (void)poly; (void)v1;

    /* Correct reduction (Gueron):
       Let the 256-bit product be [X1:X0] with X0 least significant (bit 0 = x^0) in the
       *mathematical* (not reflected) representation. Our clmul of reflected inputs
       produces the product in reflected representation, which matches GHASH if we
       reduce with the reflected polynomial.

       Reflected reduction polynomial bits: x^128 + x^7 + x^2 + x + 1 reflected is
       the same poly acting on the high half. The standard code is:

         __m128i t0 = _mm_clmulepi64_si128(X0, _mm_set_epi32(0,0,0,0x87), 0x00) ... 
       Simpler: use 32-bit schoolbook reduction which is easy to get right, and
       only used... no, bulk path needs this. Let's implement the known-good
       function from BearSSL / CRYPTOGAMS carefully.

       Known-good (from various public sources, "ghash_multiply"):

         static inline __m128i ghash_red(__m128i X0, __m128i X1) {
             __m128i t = _mm_srli_epi64(X0, 63);
