       We want to eliminate product bits 0..127 (the lo half), replacing each by poly<<128.
       (lo bits 0..63)*poly has degree <= 70, and represents values at positions 128..198.
       Those map into hi bits 0..70. xor r_lo into hi. No overflow past bit 255.
       (lo bits 64..127)*poly represents positions (64+128)..(127+7+128) = 192..262.
       r_hi bit j represents position 192+j.
       hi bit j represents position 128+j.
       So r_hi bit j -> hi bit (j+64). Shift left 64. Overflow r_hi bits 64..70 represent
       positions 256..262, i.e. 128+0 .. 128+6 plus 128 (one more reduction): position
       256+t = x^{256+t} = x^{128} * x^{128+t} ≡ poly * x^{128+t}, so it should be xored
       into position 128+t (hi bit t) AND that is not the end if we only xor poly once...
       x^{256+t} = x^{128+t} * x^{128} ≡ x^{128+t} * poly = x^{128+t}*(x^7+x^2+x+1)
       = x^{135+t} + x^{130+t} + x^{129+t} + x^{128+t}.
       For t=0..6 these are positions 128..142, all within hi (128..255). So we must xor
       (r_hi >> 64) * poly into hi, shifted to start at bit 0 of hi. Yes: the overflow
       (top of r_hi) times poly, xored into hi. Equivalently do a second reduction pass
       on the bits of hi that came from beyond 255 — but simpler to just take r_hi bits
       64..70, multiply by poly, xor into hi at bit 0.

       Implementation: 
         hi ^= r_lo
         hi ^= r_hi << 64
         overflow = r_hi >> 64          (only bits 0..6 meaningful)
         hi ^= overflow * poly
    */
    __m128i overflow = _mm_srli_si128(r_hi, 8); /* this is bits 64..127 of r_hi, but r_hi's high 64 is bits 64..127. srli_si128 by 8 moves them to low lane. */
    __m128i r_ov = _mm_clmulepi64_si128(overflow, poly, 0x00);
